import type {ResearchPost} from './fleet-data';

export const november22ResearchBatch:readonly ResearchPost[]=[{"slug":"callcenteroutsourced-how-should-a-contact-center-audit-the-accuracy-of-its-call-dispositions","title":"How should a contact center audit the accuracy of its call dispositions?","excerpt":"A disposition audit that defines the unit, draws a reproducible sample, scores the code against the call record, and reports the error pattern.","published":"2026-11-22","sourceDate":"2026-11-22","updated":"2026-11-22","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","body":"This brief asks how a contact center should audit the accuracy of its call dispositions. The population is an outsourced queue that assigns a disposition code after each contact. The unit is one coded call compared with the call record and the customer's stated need. The analysis covers the unit, the sample, the scoring rule, and the report. A disposition is accurate when the code describes what happened and points to the right next owner.\n\nThe scope excludes a full review of agent performance or a redesign of the code set. It also excludes calls that were never coded because the system failed. The audit should still note those gaps, because a missing code is a different problem from a wrong one. Write the audit scope into the client agreement so the sample and the report have a stable boundary.\n\nA disposition is not a summary of the call. It is a category that drives routing, reporting, and sometimes billing. An audit that treats the code as a label for the agent's mood will miss the operational purpose. Start from the decision the code is meant to support, then ask whether the code supports it. A code that is used mainly because it closes the call quickly is a routing risk, not a neutral label."},{"heading":"What the evidence supports","body":"ISO 18295 places contact handling within a managed process with measurable results. ISO 10002 addresses complaint intake and records. GAO data-reliability guidance asks for a documented source, a test, and a stated limitation. None of these sources defines the correct disposition for a given call. They support a repeatable comparison rather than a single reviewer's opinion, so use them to define the test and the record rather than to claim that one code is legally correct.\n\nThe evidence favors a code set with inclusion and exclusion examples. A code that means different things to different agents cannot be audited with a simple accuracy rate. The audit should test the definition as well as the assignment. When a code is ambiguous, the finding belongs to the taxonomy owner, not only to the agent. The audit should quote the definition it used, because the client may revise the wording later.\n\nThe evidence also favors a sample that reflects the live queue. A review built from escalated cases will overstate the difficulty and understate the routine. A review built from one team will miss how another team uses the same code. The draw should be documented before anyone listens to a call. A sample that excludes one channel can still be valid, but the report must say which channel was left out."},{"heading":"Define the disposition unit","body":"Define the unit before sampling. A unit can be one call, one contact, or one case that spans several calls. For a voice queue, one call is the simplest unit, but a case may be the better measure when the customer's need is resolved across transfers. Choose one unit and apply it to every row in the sample. The choice should match the level at which the client makes a decision about the case.\n\nRecord the primary need, the action taken, and the outcome as separate fields. A code that mixes all three will be hard to score and harder to trend. If the client's system has only one field, the audit should state that limit. The report can still compare the code with the call, but it cannot separate cause from outcome. If the fields cannot be separated, the audit should say so before the client reads a rate.\n\nWrite the unit definition into the scoring guide. A reviewer should not have to decide case by case whether a transfer counts as a new unit. A stable definition is what makes two quarters comparable, even when the code set changes. A definition that changes mid-quarter should reset the trend line rather than blend two methods into one number. The guide should show the date it was adopted.\n\nA unit that spans several calls needs a rule for the primary code. Some queues code each call and then roll the case into a final category. The audit should ask which level the client uses for reporting. Scoring a case-level report against call-level codes will produce false errors, and the report will blame agents for a mismatch the client created."},{"heading":"Build the sample from the queue","body":"Draw the sample from the full queue, not from the calls a supervisor already reviewed. A sample built from coached calls will flatter the accuracy rate. Include shifts, channels, and tenure groups in proportion to their volume. If a group is too small to sample, say so instead of dropping it silently. A small group can be reported as a note instead of a rate so the reader knows it was not ignored.\n\nUse a random or stratified draw and keep the selection record. The record should show the date range, the filters, and the number of calls. A reviewer who cannot reproduce the sample cannot defend the result. Size the sample for the decision; a small monthly check can be enough for a trend, while a client audit may need more. Keep the random seed or the selection file so a second analyst can draw the same calls.\n\nExclude a call only for a stated reason, such as a missing recording or a code changed after the audit began. Keep the excluded count in the report. A large exclusion rate can change the conclusion more than the accuracy rate itself. If the exclusion rate is high, the audit should explain why before it reports any accuracy figure at all."},{"heading":"Score against the call record","body":"Score each call against the recording, the note, and the disposition. Read the customer's request first, then the action, then the code. Do not start with the code and look for support. That order invites confirmation bias and hides a mismatch. A reviewer who reads the code first will find a reason for it even when the call does not support it.\n\nMark a code correct only when it matches the defined inclusion rule. Mark it wrong when another code fits better, and mark it unclear when the record cannot support a decision. Keep those categories separate in the report. A high unclear rate is a documentation problem, not proof of agent error. A code can be technically allowed but still wrong for the customer's actual request.\n\nHave a second reviewer score a small overlap sample. The overlap shows whether two people apply the guide the same way. A large disagreement rate means the guide is not ready for a client report. Fix the guide before expanding the sample. Keep the overlap small, because the second review doubles the time spent on each of the calls it covers.\n\nKeep the reviewer's note short and quote the customer's request where it matters. A long narrative invites interpretation and makes the second review slow. A short note with a time stamp and the deciding fact is easier to compare across reviewers. The note should also say which record was missing when the answer is unclear. A note that quotes the customer avoids an argument about what the customer meant."},{"heading":"Separate agent error from taxonomy error","body":"A wrong code can come from the agent, the definition, or the system. If several agents use the same wrong code for the same situation, the definition is the likely cause. If one agent's errors cluster on one code, coaching may help. Review the pattern before assigning blame. Look at the code distribution before the individual calls, because a cluster is easier to see.\n\nA code that requires a fact the agent cannot see will fail regardless of training. Check whether the screen shows the needed field at the moment of coding. A system change can fix more errors than a memo. Record the cause in the finding so the client can choose the right owner. A field that exists but is hidden behind another screen has the same effect as a missing field.\n\nA third cause is the timing of the code. An agent who codes before the call ends may choose a provisional category and never update it. Check whether the system allows a later change and whether anyone reviews it. A timing rule can remove a whole class of errors. If the system does not allow a later change, the agent should wait for the call to close."},{"heading":"Report the finding with its limit","body":"Report the accuracy rate with the sample size, the date range, and the scoring rule. Show the wrong and unclear counts separately. Do not present a single percentage without the denominator. A rate from twelve calls is a signal, not a stable measure. Put the denominator and the date range in the first sentence, not in a footnote at the end.\n\nName the dominant error pattern and the owner who can fix it. State what the audit did not cover, such as calls without audio or codes changed after the fact. A report that hides its limits will be quoted as stronger than it is. The client should be able to see how the conclusion was reached. The client should see the error pattern before the rate, because the pattern suggests the fix.\n\nKeep the report to one page for the operating review and a short appendix for the method. The page should answer whether the codes can be trusted for the decision at hand. The appendix should let a new analyst repeat the work. A long report that no one reads does not improve the code set. A short report with a clear method is more likely to be used than a long one with no owner."},{"heading":"Limits and review","body":"This method cannot prove that a disposition is legally correct or that the customer's need was fully met. It measures whether the code matches the record and the definition. The queue can control the sample, the scoring, and the report. State those limits when the client asks for a single accuracy number. The report should say which decisions the rate can support and which it cannot.\n\nRepeat the audit each quarter and after a code-set change. Compare the error pattern with the previous round. If the same code keeps failing, revise the definition or the screen. A review that only reports the rate without a fix will not improve the next round. Keep the same code definitions in the comparison, or mark the break in the chart.\n\nKeep the scoring guide and the sample record together. The next auditor should start from the last method, not invent a new one. A stable method makes a small improvement visible, which is often the point of the audit. Date the guide and keep each old version so that a later dispute about the method can be resolved fully. The guide should be dated and stored with the sample."}],"sources":[{"name":"Occupational Outlook Handbook: Customer Service Representatives","organization":"Bureau of Labor Statistics","date":"2026","url":"https://www.bls.gov/ooh/office-and-administrative-support/customer-service-representatives.htm","note":"Employment, wage, training, and outlook figures for customer service representatives."},{"name":"GAO-20-283G: Assessing Data Reliability","organization":"U.S. Government Accountability Office","date":"2020","url":"https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-20-283g","note":"A practical framework for documenting data sources, testing reliability, and stating limitations."},{"name":"ISO 18295-1:2017 Customer contact centres","organization":"International Organization for Standardization","date":"2017","url":"https://www.iso.org/standard/64739.html","note":"Requirements for customer contact centre processes, people, and performance."},{"name":"ISO 10002:2018 Quality management - Customer satisfaction - Guidelines for complaints handling","organization":"International Organization for Standardization","date":"2018","url":"https://www.iso.org/standard/71536.html","note":"Guidelines for complaint intake, response, and records in customer-facing organizations."},{"name":"NIST Special Publication 800-92: Guide to Computer Security Log Management","organization":"National Institute of Standards and Technology","date":"2006","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/92/final","note":"Guidance on log sources, retention, review, and the limits of log evidence."}],"keyStats":["2.67 million U.S. customer service representative jobs in 2025 (BLS)","$21.53 median hourly wage in May 2025 (BLS)","289,500 projected annual openings, 2025 to 2035 (BLS)"],"takeaways":["Define the disposition unit and the scoring rule before drawing the sample.","Draw from the full queue and keep a reproducible selection record.","Score the code against the customer's request, the action, and the outcome.","Separate agent error from ambiguous definitions and missing screen fields.","Report the rate with its sample size, unclear count, and limits."],"faqs":[{"question":"How large should a disposition audit sample be?","answer":"Size it for the decision. A small monthly sample can show a trend, while a client audit or a code-set change may need a larger draw. Report the sample size with every rate."},{"question":"What if the call has no recording?","answer":"Mark it unclear only when the note cannot support a decision, and count it in the report. A missing recording is a documentation gap, not proof that the agent coded wrongly."}],"related":["callcenteroutsourced-how-complete-is-the-disposition-code-evidence","callcenteroutsourced-when-does-a-quality-sample-stop-supporting-a-comparison-between-two-vendor-teams"],"internalLinks":["/research","/services","/contact-us"],"serviceHandoff":{"href":"/contact-us","label":"Review the disposition audit","body":"Bring the current code definitions, one month of coded calls, and the last audit so the review can test the sample and the scoring guide."},"image":"/daily22/callcenteroutsourced/callcenteroutsourced-how-should-a-contact-center-audit-the-accuracy-of-its-call-dispositions.webp"},{"slug":"callcenteroutsourced-what-should-a-contact-center-record-when-it-denies-a-refund-request","title":"What should a contact center record when it denies a refund request?","excerpt":"A denial record that names the rule, lists the evidence reviewed, states what the customer heard, and keeps the appeal path visible.","published":"2026-11-22","sourceDate":"2026-11-22","updated":"2026-11-22","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","body":"This brief asks what a contact center should record when it denies a refund request. The population is an outsourced queue that handles billing contacts for a client. The unit is one denial communicated to a customer during a call. The analysis covers the reason, the evidence reviewed, the message given, and the appeal path. A denial record should let a later reviewer understand the decision without listening to the whole call.\n\nThe scope excludes the client's refund policy and any legal question about a charge. It also excludes refunds that are approved and then reversed by a payment processor. The queue's record is an operational account of what it checked and what it told the customer. Write that role into the client agreement so the queue does not appear to be the final decision maker.\n\nA denial is not the end of the interaction. The customer may appeal, ask for a supervisor, or dispute the charge with an issuer. The record should anticipate those paths without pretending to decide them. A short, factual note reduces the chance that the next contact starts from zero. The note should also name the channel the customer used, because an appeal may arrive by a different one."},{"heading":"What the evidence supports","body":"ISO 10002 treats complaint handling as a process with a response and a record. ISO 18295 places contact work within a managed system. GAO data-reliability guidance asks for a source and a limitation when a decision rests on data. None of these sources defines when a refund should be denied. They support a record that shows the rule and the facts rather than only the outcome.\n\nThe evidence favors a reason that names the rule, not a phrase like not eligible. A reason that cannot be checked is hard to appeal and easy to apply unfairly. The record should also show which facts the agent reviewed, such as the order date or the delivery status. That detail separates a policy decision from an agent's mood. The rule reference should be the version that applied on the call date, not the current one.\n\nThe evidence also favors a record the customer can understand. A note written only in internal codes may satisfy the file but fail the customer. The queue should be able to read the reason back in plain language when the customer asks for an explanation. A plain-language reason should still match the internal category, or the two records will diverge."},{"heading":"Name the denial reason","body":"Use the client's own reason categories and quote the rule that applies. If the policy says a refund is available within thirty days, record the order date and the request date. Do not paraphrase the rule in a way that changes the threshold. A precise reason is the base for every later review. If the policy changed recently, record which version the agent used.\n\nWhen more than one reason applies, record the primary one and note the others. A customer who hears only one reason may appeal on that point, so the record should show the full basis. Do not pile on reasons to make the denial sound stronger. Use the reasons that actually drove the decision. The primary reason should be the one that would still deny the request if the others fell away.\n\nIf the reason depends on a fact the customer disputes, say so. A denial built on a delivery date the customer rejects needs a note about the dispute. The appeal reviewer can then check the source instead of assuming the fact was settled. The appeal owner should see both the customer's account of the fact and the source the agent used.\n\nA denial reason should be stable enough to trend. If the queue renames a category every quarter, the client cannot see whether denials are rising. Keep the old label in the history and map it to the new one. A trend line with a hidden rename is worse than no trend line. A mapping table should be kept with the reason list so old reports can still be read."},{"heading":"Record the evidence reviewed","body":"List the records the agent checked and the result of each check. Include the order, the payment, the delivery, and any prior contact that affects the decision. Do not copy sensitive data into the note; reference the record instead. The reviewer should be able to follow the same path without asking the agent to remember. A reference is better than a pasted screenshot because the source can be checked later.\n\nIf a record was missing, say so. A denial based on an absent record is different from one based on a clear policy limit. The missing record may also be the reason to escalate rather than deny. Recording the gap protects the customer and gives the client a fixable issue. The escalation should happen before the denial is sent, not after the customer appeals.\n\nDate each check when the system allows it. A delivery status that changes after the call can make a correct denial look wrong in hindsight. The timestamp shows what the agent could see at the moment of the decision. If the system does not time-stamp the check, the agent should write the exact time into the case text itself. A date field is better than a free-text note.\n\nCheck that the reason list is short enough to use under pressure. A list with forty categories invites the agent to pick the first plausible one. Group the rare reasons under a parent category and require a note. The reviewer can then see the detail without making the live call slow. The parent category should still require a short note so the detail is not lost."},{"heading":"State what the customer was told","body":"Record the message the customer heard, not only the internal reason. Include the rule, the next step, and any date the customer was given. If the customer was offered a credit or an alternative, note it. The spoken message and the note should not tell two different stories. If the customer was given a date, the note should say who owns that date.\n\nUse neutral wording that describes the decision rather than judging the customer. Avoid phrases that suggest the customer was dishonest or careless unless the evidence supports that finding. A denial can be firm without being insulting. The record may be read by the customer in a later request. A neutral note also protects the agent if the customer later disputes the tone of the call.\n\nIf the customer was upset, record the escalation and the response, not a character judgment. A note that says the customer was angry tells the next agent very little. A note that says the customer asked for a supervisor and was offered a callback tells the next agent what to do. The escalation note should record what the supervisor said, not only that a transfer occurred."},{"heading":"Keep the appeal path","body":"Name the appeal route and the owner who will review it. Record the deadline and the channel the customer should use. If the customer asks for a supervisor, note the request and the time. An appeal path that exists only in a policy document is not a path the customer can use. The route should be written where the agent can see it during the call, not in a policy binder.\n\nDo not promise a different outcome to end the call. Say that the appeal will be reviewed by a named role. If the queue cannot accept an appeal by phone, say which channel can. Record the customer's choice so the next agent does not restart the explanation. If the customer chooses a written appeal, record the address or portal and the date sent.\n\nCheck that the appeal owner has the records needed to decide. An appeal that begins with a missing order number will stall. Send the case with the evidence list, not only the denial reason. A complete evidence list is far more useful to the appeal owner than a long written explanation of why the original denial was considered correct. The list should travel with the case.\n\nRecord the customer's preferred remedy when they state one. A customer who asked for a replacement rather than a refund may accept a different outcome. The note lets the appeal owner see whether the denial addressed the actual request. It also prevents the next agent from offering a remedy the customer already rejected. The remedy should be recorded as a preference, not as an entitlement the queue has accepted."},{"heading":"Separate policy from agent judgment","body":"Mark whether the denial followed a written rule or relied on an agent's judgment. A judgment call needs a supervisor review and a clearer note. A rule-based denial needs the rule reference and the facts. Mixing the two makes it hard to tell whether the queue applied the policy or invented it. The label should be visible in the report so a reader can see how the decision was made.\n\nIf the agent departed from the rule, record the exception and the approver. Do not hide an exception inside a general note. Exceptions are legitimate when the client authorizes them, but they must be visible. A pattern of undocumented exceptions is a policy problem. An exception that becomes common should be written into the rule instead of approved case by case.\n\nReview judgment calls more closely than rule-based denials. A judgment call has more room for inconsistency, and the customer is more likely to feel treated unfairly. A second reviewer can confirm the reasoning before the appeal arrives. The second reviewer should write a short reason for agreement or disagreement, not only a simple yes or no at the end of the review."},{"heading":"Limits and review","body":"This record cannot decide whether the client's policy is fair or lawful. It also cannot replace a payment processor's dispute process. The queue can record the rule, the evidence, and the message. State those limits when a customer asks the queue to overrule the policy. The client should know that the queue is describing a decision, not defending the policy itself.\n\nCount denials by reason, product, and shift. A reason that appears far more often on one shift may point to training or to a rule that is unclear. A reason that appears across all shifts may point to the policy itself. Compare the counts with approvals to see the full picture. A reason that appears in only one product may point to a rule written for a different product.\n\nSample a few denials each month and check the record against the call. Look for missing evidence, a vague reason, or a customer who was not told about the appeal. Share the pattern with the client without naming customers. The review should end with one rule or one script to fix. The review should name the one fix and the date the next sample will check it."}],"sources":[{"name":"Occupational Outlook Handbook: Customer Service Representatives","organization":"Bureau of Labor Statistics","date":"2026","url":"https://www.bls.gov/ooh/office-and-administrative-support/customer-service-representatives.htm","note":"Employment, wage, training, and outlook figures for customer service representatives."},{"name":"ISO 10002:2018 Quality management - Customer satisfaction - Guidelines for complaints handling","organization":"International Organization for Standardization","date":"2018","url":"https://www.iso.org/standard/71536.html","note":"Guidelines for complaint intake, response, and records in customer-facing organizations."},{"name":"ISO 18295-1:2017 Customer contact centres","organization":"International Organization for Standardization","date":"2017","url":"https://www.iso.org/standard/64739.html","note":"Requirements for customer contact centre processes, people, and performance."},{"name":"GAO-20-283G: Assessing Data Reliability","organization":"U.S. Government Accountability Office","date":"2020","url":"https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-20-283g","note":"A practical framework for documenting data sources, testing reliability, and stating limitations."},{"name":"NIST Special Publication 800-92: Guide to Computer Security Log Management","organization":"National Institute of Standards and Technology","date":"2006","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/92/final","note":"Guidance on log sources, retention, review, and the limits of log evidence."}],"keyStats":["2.67 million U.S. customer service representative jobs in 2025 (BLS)","$21.53 median hourly wage in May 2025 (BLS)","289,500 projected annual openings, 2025 to 2035 (BLS)"],"takeaways":["Name the rule and the facts that drove the denial, not just the outcome.","List the records checked and note any record that was missing.","Record what the customer was told and which appeal route was offered.","Mark rule-based and judgment-based denials differently.","Review denial patterns by reason, product, and shift."],"faqs":[{"question":"Should the queue tell the customer the internal reason code?","answer":"It should give a plain-language reason that matches the rule. Internal codes can stay in the note, but the customer needs an explanation they can understand and appeal."},{"question":"What if the customer disputes a fact in the denial?","answer":"Record the dispute, note the source the agent relied on, and route the case to the appeal owner. Do not repeat the disputed fact as settled."}],"related":["callcenteroutsourced-where-does-refund-request-routing-evidence-break-across-a-shift","callcenteroutsourced-what-does-a-quality-calibration-record-need-to-survive-a-client-audit"],"internalLinks":["/research","/services","/contact-us"],"serviceHandoff":{"href":"/contact-us","label":"Review the refund denial record","body":"Bring the current reason list, two closed denials, and the appeal route so the review can check the evidence and the customer message."},"image":"/daily22/callcenteroutsourced/callcenteroutsourced-what-should-a-contact-center-record-when-it-denies-a-refund-request.webp"},{"slug":"callcenteroutsourced-how-should-a-contact-center-test-screen-reader-access-to-its-agent-tools","title":"How should a contact center test screen-reader access to its agent tools?","excerpt":"A task-based test that checks whether an agent can answer, note, and close a call with a screen reader, and records every blocker with an owner.","published":"2026-11-22","sourceDate":"2026-11-22","updated":"2026-11-22","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","body":"This brief asks how a contact center should test whether its agent tools work with a screen reader. The population is an outsourced queue whose agents use a telephony console, a CRM, and a knowledge panel. The unit is one task performed with a screen reader on the live tool set. The analysis covers the task list, the test method, the blocker record, and the retest.\n\nThe scope excludes a full accessibility audit of the client's public website. It also excludes a legal determination about a specific accommodation. The queue's test is operational: can an agent who relies on a screen reader complete the task at the same service standard? Write that question into the client agreement so a fix has an owner. A person who does not use a screen reader every day can still run the session, but a regular user should confirm the result.\n\nThe test should involve a person who uses the tool with a screen reader, not only a developer with a checker. Automated scans can find missing labels, but they do not show whether a live call can be answered. The queue should treat the test as a work trial, not a compliance document. The test should be repeated after a major browser update, because focus behavior can change without a tool release."},{"heading":"What the evidence supports","body":"DOL guidance on employment technology asks employers to consider how tools are used by people with disabilities. NIST identity and privacy guidance supports controlled access without excluding a legitimate user. ISO 18295 places contact handling in a managed process. None of these sources lists the exact keys or labels a tool must have. They support a task-based test with a named owner.\n\nThe evidence favors testing the real workflow rather than a sample page. A screen reader may read a button correctly and still fail when a call arrives. The test should include the interruption, the note, and the wrap-up. A tool that passes a static check can still block an agent during a live contact. The test should follow the order an agent actually works, because a shortcut can hide a failure in the normal path.\n\nA useful standard is independence. The agent should complete the task without a colleague reading the screen. If a helper is needed, the task is not accessible at that moment. Record the helper step as a blocker, even when the workaround is quick. A workaround that needs a sighted colleague is evidence of a gap, even when the customer never notices."},{"heading":"Choose the tool paths to test","body":"List the tasks an agent performs in a typical shift. Include login, call answer, hold, transfer, note entry, disposition, and logout. Rank them by whether the queue can operate without them. A tool that fails at login is a harder blocker than one that fails on a rare report. The task list should be short enough to repeat each quarter without a large project.\n\nTest the paths that carry the highest volume and the highest risk. A transfer that cannot be completed with a screen reader can strand a customer. A report that takes longer is a productivity issue, not always a service blocker. Separate the two so the fix list has a priority. A low-volume task can still be a blocker if it is the only way to finish a call.\n\nInclude the error states. A screen reader user needs to hear a failed save or a dropped call as clearly as a success. Test with a slow connection and a timed-out session. The error path is where accessibility gaps often hide. The error path should be tested with the same care as the happy path, because a failed save can lose a case.\n\nTest the handoff between tools, not only each tool alone. A screen reader may work in the CRM and fail when the call console opens a new window. The agent experiences one workflow, so the test should follow that workflow across the boundary. A per-tool checklist can miss the exact moment where focus is lost. The handoff test should include the moment focus moves from one window to another."},{"heading":"Run the test with a real task","body":"Give the tester a scripted case with a known outcome. Ask them to answer, verify, note, and close it using only the screen reader. Observe where they pause, repeat, or ask for help. Do not prompt unless the session is about to fail for a reason outside the tool. The tester should be allowed to stop the session if the tool causes a real error or a lost note.\n\nRecord the time and the number of keystrokes for each step. Compare the result with the queue's normal standard, not with a perfect run. A task that takes twice as long may still be workable if the queue can staff for it. A task that cannot be completed is a blocker regardless of speed. The time comparison should use the queue's standard for the task, not a stopwatch held by the tool owner.\n\nRun the test on the same build the floor uses. A staging version can hide a missing label that production adds, or the reverse. Note the browser, the screen reader, and the operating system. A result without those details is hard to reproduce. The build reference should include the version number, the date the floor received it, and the browser used."},{"heading":"Record the blocker","body":"Write the blocker as a task, not as a complaint. State the step, what the screen reader announced, and what the agent needed. Include a screenshot only when it adds information the words cannot. Send the record to the tool owner with the build reference. The note should say whether the blocker stops the task or only slows it down.\n\nRank the blocker by service impact. A missing label on a rarely used field is lower than a transfer control that cannot be reached. The owner needs the rank to schedule the fix. Do not send a long list without priority and expect the team to guess. The rank should be agreed with the supervisor who owns the affected queue.\n\nKeep a copy of the blocker log with the client file. A fix that is promised but not tested should stay open. The log should show the date found, the date fixed, and the date retested. That history is the evidence that the queue did not only report the issue. The log should be reviewed in the client meeting, not only stored in a shared folder.\n\nAsk the tester to narrate what they hear, not what they expect. The narration shows whether the label matches the action. A button called submit may announce a save, and the mismatch can cause a wrong click. Record the announcement in the blocker note. The announcement should be quoted exactly, because a paraphrase can hide the mismatch between the label and the action."},{"heading":"Fix and retest","body":"Retest the same task after the fix, with the same screen reader and build. A fix that works in one browser may fail in another. Ask the tester to complete the task without help and record the result. Close the blocker only when the task is independent. The retest should use a different tester when possible, so the first tester's familiarity does not mask a gap.\n\nIf the fix changes the layout, test the adjacent tasks as well. A new button can shift the focus order and break a path that used to work. Keep the retest short but repeatable. The goal is confidence, not a full regression suite. The adjacent test should include the task before and after the changed step in the same session.\n\nWhen a vendor cannot fix the tool, look for a process workaround with a named owner. A second device or a different role may complete the step, but the workaround should be documented and reviewed. A permanent workaround is a design choice, not a solution. The workaround should have an expiry date so it does not become the permanent process."},{"heading":"Train the floor","body":"Tell supervisors and agents what the tool can and cannot do with a screen reader. A supervisor who does not know the limit may blame the agent for a slow task. Include the approved workaround and the person who owns it. Training should be short and repeated when the tool changes. The training should include the exact keys the agent needs for the task, not a general tour of the tool.\n\nAsk the tester to help write the guide. Their experience is more useful than a generic checklist. Keep the guide focused on the tasks the queue actually performs. A guide that describes every menu will not be read during a shift. The guide should be reviewed by the tester before it is published to the floor or used in training.\n\nReview accommodation requests through the normal HR process, not through the queue's informal fixes. The queue can supply the task evidence, but it should not decide the accommodation alone. That boundary protects the agent and the client. The accommodation decision should be recorded by HR, with the queue's evidence attached. The decision should be kept with the same task evidence.\n\nSet a target date for each high-impact blocker and review it in the client meeting. A fix without a date tends to wait for a larger release. The queue should not promise the date, but it should ask for one. An open blocker with no date is a risk the client has not accepted. The target date should be reviewed at the next client meeting, even if the fix has not started."},{"heading":"Limits and review","body":"This test cannot certify a tool as fully accessible. It also cannot replace a legal review of an accommodation request. The queue can control the task list, the observation, and the blocker record. State those limits when a client asks for a compliance statement. The report should say which tasks were tested and which tasks were not tested at all in this round.\n\nRepeat the test when a tool is upgraded or a new screen reader version is deployed. Sample one task per quarter even without a change. Review the blocker log for items that stayed open too long. A test that is never repeated becomes a historical note. The test should be repeated after a new screen reader version, even when the tool has not changed.\n\nKeep the results with the client review pack. The client should see the open blockers and the dates they were reported. A short, current list is more useful than a large report written once. The next test should start from the last open item. The open blocker list should be short enough to read at the start of a review."}],"sources":[{"name":"Fact Sheet #64: Call Centers under the Fair Labor Standards Act","organization":"U.S. Department of Labor, Wage and Hour Division","date":"2008","url":"https://www.dol.gov/agencies/whd/fact-sheets/64-flsa-call-centers","note":"Hours-worked, rest-period, and recordkeeping rules for call center employees."},{"name":"ISO 18295-1:2017 Customer contact centres","organization":"International Organization for Standardization","date":"2017","url":"https://www.iso.org/standard/64739.html","note":"Requirements for customer contact centre processes, people, and performance."},{"name":"ISO 9001:2015 Quality management systems","organization":"International Organization for Standardization","date":"2015","url":"https://www.iso.org/standard/62085.html","note":"Process-control and documented-information requirements for a quality management system."},{"name":"NIST Special Publication 800-63B: Digital Identity Guidelines","organization":"National Institute of Standards and Technology","date":"2017","url":"https://pages.nist.gov/800-63-3/sp800-63b.html","note":"Authentication and identity-proofing guidance relevant to verifying a caller before account actions."},{"name":"Occupational Outlook Handbook: Customer Service Representatives","organization":"Bureau of Labor Statistics","date":"2026","url":"https://www.bls.gov/ooh/office-and-administrative-support/customer-service-representatives.htm","note":"Employment, wage, training, and outlook figures for customer service representatives."}],"keyStats":["2.67 million U.S. customer service representative jobs in 2025 (BLS)","$21.53 median hourly wage in May 2025 (BLS)","289,500 projected annual openings, 2025 to 2035 (BLS)"],"takeaways":["Test the live tool set with a person who uses a screen reader.","Rank blockers by whether the queue can operate without the task.","Close a blocker only after an independent retest on the production build.","Keep the blocker log with dates found, fixed, and retested."],"faqs":[{"question":"Can an automated accessibility scan replace the test?","answer":"No. A scan can find missing labels, but it cannot show whether an agent can answer a live call, transfer, and close it without help."},{"question":"Who owns a blocker in a vendor tool?","answer":"The tool owner named in the client agreement. The queue supplies the task evidence and the build reference, and the client tracks the fix date."}],"related":["callcenteroutsourced-what-should-a-contact-center-measure-when-a-client-moves-work-to-self-service","callcenteroutsourced-how-should-a-contact-center-evaluate-a-live-chat-translation-tool"],"internalLinks":["/research","/services","/contact-us"],"serviceHandoff":{"href":"/contact-us","label":"Review the screen-reader test","body":"Bring the task list, the current blocker log, and one recorded test session so the review can check the method and the retest."},"image":"/daily22/callcenteroutsourced/callcenteroutsourced-how-should-a-contact-center-test-screen-reader-access-to-its-agent-tools.webp"},{"slug":"callcenteroutsourced-how-should-a-contact-center-set-a-service-level-for-a-queue-with-mandatory-authentication","title":"How should a contact center set a service level for a queue with mandatory authentication?","excerpt":"A two-clock service level that separates queue wait from identity verification, reports both, and tests the staffing effect before the target is fixed.","published":"2026-11-22","sourceDate":"2026-11-22","updated":"2026-11-22","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","body":"This brief asks how a contact center should set a service level for a queue where every caller must be authenticated. The population is an outsourced queue that handles account work for a client. The unit is one inbound contact that includes an identity check before service. The analysis covers the measured interval, the target, the report, and the staffing effect.\n\nThe scope excludes the client's identity-proofing standard, which sets what counts as verified. It also excludes contacts that arrive already authenticated through a trusted app. The queue's question is narrower: how long should a customer wait when the verification step is part of the service? The queue can state what it needs from the client without redefining the assurance level.\n\nA single service level that ignores authentication will compare unlike calls. A queue with a two-minute check and a queue with no check can post the same number and deliver different experiences. The target should make the difference visible rather than hide it in an average. The target should show the verification cost as a separate line, not spread across every call."},{"heading":"What the evidence supports","body":"NIST Special Publication 800-63B describes authentication assurance and the need to match a method to the risk of the action. ISO 18295 places contact performance within a managed process. GAO data-reliability guidance asks for a defined measure and a stated limitation. None of these sources sets a service-level number for a queue. The sources support a definition and a record, not a claim that one wait time is correct for every queue.\n\nThe evidence supports measuring the customer's total wait, not only the queue wait. A caller who reaches an agent quickly and then spends four minutes verifying has not received a fast answer. The report should show both intervals so the client can see where the time went. The two intervals should be labelled so a reader cannot confuse one with the other.\n\nThe evidence also favors separating the authentication step from the service step in the target. A queue can meet a service level for answer speed and still fail on end-to-end time. The client should choose which measure drives the contract and which one drives improvement. The contract should say which interval the client is buying and which one is only for improvement."},{"heading":"Define the measured interval","body":"Name the start and end of the interval. The start can be the moment the call enters the queue or the moment the customer finishes the IVR. The end can be the first agent greeting or the completion of verification. Each choice produces a different number. The definition should be written before the first report, not after a dispute about the number.\n\nFor a customer-facing promise, use the full interval from arrival to the start of the actual service. That includes the wait and the check. For a staffing measure, use the queue wait before the agent answers. Report both and label them clearly. The interval should include the time the customer spends waiting for the check to begin, not only the check itself.\n\nKeep the definition stable across channels. A chat authentication step may happen before the queue, while a voice check happens after the greeting. If the channel changes the definition, say so in the report. A comparison across channels is only fair when the intervals match. The channel note should be visible in the comparison so a reader does not assume identical starts.\n\nA service level should also name the population it covers. A queue that serves several clients may have different verification rules for each. Report the measure by client and by contact type. A blended number can hide one client whose check is much slower. The population note should also say when a client is excluded from the measure and why."},{"heading":"Separate authentication from queue wait","body":"Capture the time spent in verification as its own field. Do not bury it in talk time or after-call work. The field should start when the agent begins the check and end when the identity is accepted or the call is routed. A clear field lets the queue see the cost of the step. The field should be visible on the report the supervisor uses during the shift.\n\nSome checks are quick and some require a callback or a document. Those cases should not sit in the same bucket as a standard check. Split the report by verification type so a slow exception does not distort the normal path. The split should include the method name so a change in method is visible in the trend. The method name should be stable across reports.\n\nReview the split with the client's identity owner. If one method is slow and rarely needed, the client may move it to a different channel. The queue's data can support that decision without the queue setting the policy. The identity owner should see the split before the client changes the verification rule. The queue should record the decision and its date.\n\nTest the definition with a timed call before publishing it. Run one contact through the queue and mark the start and end by hand. If two observers choose different points, the definition is not clear enough. Fix the wording before the number reaches a contract. The timed call should include a failed check and a second attempt after the failure."},{"heading":"Set the target by contact type","body":"Set a target for simple authenticated contacts and a different target for complex ones. A password reset and a fraud review should not share one number. The customer's need and the risk of the action determine the reasonable wait. The target should be written as a range for the customer, not only as an internal average. The range should be wide enough to be honest.\n\nUse the client's promise as the outer limit and the queue's history as the working target. A target that no shift has met for a month is not a target; it is a wish. A target that every shift meets without effort may be too loose to protect the customer. The target should be reviewed when the client adds a new account type or a new risk tier.\n\nState the target in the contract with the measure and the exclusions. Name what happens when a system outage or a provider failure affects the check. An exclusion that is too broad can hide a real gap. Review the exclusions each quarter. The exclusion should name the evidence the queue will use to show that an outage occurred and was outside the queue's control.\n\nCapture the number of verification attempts, not only the time. A caller who fails twice and succeeds on the third try has a different experience from one who passes immediately. The attempt count explains a long verification time better than an average. The attempt count should be reported with the time so a long check is not mistaken for a slow agent."},{"heading":"Report the two clocks","body":"Show the queue clock and the end-to-end clock in the same report. The queue clock shows staffing pressure; the end-to-end clock shows the customer's experience. A gap between them points to the authentication step or to after-call work. The two clocks should use the same date range and the same contact population. The same population should be used for every comparison.\n\nAdd the abandonment point to the report. A caller who leaves during verification is different from one who leaves before an agent answers. The first may indicate a confusing check, while the second may indicate a long wait. Treat them as separate signals. The abandonment point should say which step the customer left, not only that the call ended at some point.\n\nKeep the report short enough for a weekly review. One table with the two clocks and the verification split is enough. A long dashboard can hide the one number the client needs. The review should end with one owner and one action. The table should show the owner of each number so the review has a clear place to start."},{"heading":"Test the effect on staffing","body":"Model the authentication time as part of the workload, not as a fixed overhead. If the check takes two minutes, the agent needs two more minutes per contact in the plan. A staffing model that ignores the step will understate the requirement. The model should separate the check from the service work even when one agent performs both. The split should be visible in the weekly report.\n\nTest the model against a week of actual data before changing the roster. Compare the planned minutes with the observed minutes by contact type. If the gap is large, check whether the verification method changed during the week. The weekly comparison should use the same contact types that the model covers. The comparison should cover the same week for both plans.\n\nShare the result with the client before adding staff. A client may prefer to simplify the check rather than pay for more agents. The queue should present both options with the service effect. That conversation is easier with two clocks than with one average. The client should see the staffing effect of the current check before approving a longer one.\n\nShow the two clocks by hour of day as well as by week. A queue can meet its weekly target and still fail every Monday morning. The hourly view shows when the authentication step collides with the arrival peak. That is the view a supervisor can act on. The hourly view should mark the arrival peak and the verification peak on the same chart."},{"heading":"Limits and review","body":"This method cannot decide whether an authentication method is strong enough for a given action. It also cannot remove the legal or policy need for a check. The queue can measure the wait, separate the step, and report the effect. State those limits when the client asks for a single service-level number. The queue should not present a measure as a promise when the client has not approved the target.\n\nReview the target when the client changes its identity method or its channel mix. Recheck the two clocks after the change. Sample a few calls to confirm the verification field is accurate. A measure is only useful when the floor records it the same way. The sample should include a call from each verification method that is currently in use.\n\nKeep the definition and the exclusions in the client agreement. A target that lives only in a slide will drift with each review. Date every version and note what changed. The next review should start from the last definition, not from memory. The agreement should say who may change the definition and how the change is announced to the floor.\n\nCheck the model against a day with an outage or a provider delay. A plan that only works in normal conditions will fail when the check slows down. Add a sensitivity line for a longer verification time. The client can then see the cost of the policy it chooses. The sensitivity line should show the effect of a one-minute increase in verification time."}],"sources":[{"name":"NIST Special Publication 800-63B: Digital Identity Guidelines","organization":"National Institute of Standards and Technology","date":"2017","url":"https://pages.nist.gov/800-63-3/sp800-63b.html","note":"Authentication and identity-proofing guidance relevant to verifying a caller before account actions."},{"name":"ISO 18295-1:2017 Customer contact centres","organization":"International Organization for Standardization","date":"2017","url":"https://www.iso.org/standard/64739.html","note":"Requirements for customer contact centre processes, people, and performance."},{"name":"Occupational Outlook Handbook: Customer Service Representatives","organization":"Bureau of Labor Statistics","date":"2026","url":"https://www.bls.gov/ooh/office-and-administrative-support/customer-service-representatives.htm","note":"Employment, wage, training, and outlook figures for customer service representatives."},{"name":"GAO-20-283G: Assessing Data Reliability","organization":"U.S. Government Accountability Office","date":"2020","url":"https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-20-283g","note":"A practical framework for documenting data sources, testing reliability, and stating limitations."},{"name":"ISO 9001:2015 Quality management systems","organization":"International Organization for Standardization","date":"2015","url":"https://www.iso.org/standard/62085.html","note":"Process-control and documented-information requirements for a quality management system."}],"keyStats":["2.67 million U.S. customer service representative jobs in 2025 (BLS)","$21.53 median hourly wage in May 2025 (BLS)","289,500 projected annual openings, 2025 to 2035 (BLS)"],"takeaways":["Measure both the queue wait and the end-to-end wait after authentication.","Split verification time into its own field and report it by method.","Set different targets for simple and complex authenticated contacts.","Model authentication as workload before changing the roster.","Put the definition and exclusions in the client agreement."],"faqs":[{"question":"Should authentication time count against the service level?","answer":"It should be visible either way. For a customer-facing promise, include it in the end-to-end clock; for a staffing measure, report it separately from the queue wait."},{"question":"What if the client wants one number?","answer":"Give the client the end-to-end number and keep the queue clock as a supporting measure. A single number should not hide a slow verification step."}],"related":["callcenteroutsourced-how-should-a-contact-center-verify-a-caller-before-an-account-change","callcenteroutsourced-what-does-a-customer-identity-verification-failure-record-prove-under-review"],"internalLinks":["/research","/services","/contact-us"],"serviceHandoff":{"href":"/contact-us","label":"Review the authentication service level","body":"Bring the current definition, one week of queue and verification times, and the staffing model so the review can test both clocks."},"image":"/daily22/callcenteroutsourced/callcenteroutsourced-how-should-a-contact-center-set-a-service-level-for-a-queue-with-mandatory-authentication.webp"},{"slug":"callcenteroutsourced-when-does-a-customers-preferred-contact-time-become-a-queue-commitment","title":"When does a customer's preferred contact time become a queue commitment?","excerpt":"A callback rule that separates a stated preference from a booked promise, names the owner, and measures the windows the queue actually keeps.","published":"2026-11-22","sourceDate":"2026-11-22","updated":"2026-11-22","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","body":"This brief asks when a customer's preferred contact time becomes a commitment the queue must keep. The population is an outsourced queue that schedules callbacks and follow-ups for a client. The unit is one stated preference that the agent records during a contact. The analysis covers how the preference is captured, who may promise it, and how performance is reported.\n\nThe scope excludes a customer's general availability, such as a preference for mornings. It also excludes a marketing consent to receive calls at a certain hour. The queue's question is whether a specific window was promised, by whom, and in what record. The answer should say whether the preference is a request or a condition of service. That answer should be recorded with the preference.\n\nA preference becomes a commitment when the queue turns it into a booked contact with an owner. Until then it is useful context. The distinction matters because a missed preference is a disappointment, while a missed commitment is a broken promise. The record should show the customer's words and the queue's answer in separate fields. The two fields should not be collapsed into one note."},{"heading":"What the evidence supports","body":"ISO 10002 treats complaint handling as a process with a response and a record. ISO 18295 places contact work in a managed system with defined responsibilities. NIST log guidance supports reconstructing what was promised and when. None of these sources defines a callback window or a grace period. The sources support a written promise and a record, not a universal grace period.\n\nThe evidence favors recording the customer's words and the queue's answer separately. The customer may say any time after three, while the agent may promise a call between three and five. The record should show both so a later reviewer can see the difference. The record should show the time the promise was made, not only the time it is due.\n\nThe evidence also favors a single owner for each booked contact. A promise without an owner is a hope. A booked task with a time, a channel, and an owner is a commitment the queue can measure. The owner should be a person or a named queue, not a general team inbox. A general inbox cannot own a specific time."},{"heading":"Capture the preference exactly","body":"Write the customer's time window in their own terms and convert it to the queue's time zone. Note the date and the channel. If the customer says morning, ask for the range they mean. A vague preference should not be turned into a precise promise without confirmation. The conversion should be shown to the customer in their own zone so they can check it.\n\nRead the window back and ask the customer to confirm it. Include the day, the start, the end, and the number that will be called. If the customer cannot be reached at that number, record the alternative. A confirmation prevents a dispute about what was said. The confirmation should include what the customer should do if the call does not arrive.\n\nDo not round a preference to the nearest standard slot without telling the customer. A slot that starts an hour later may be outside the window the customer agreed to. If the queue must use slots, say so before the customer confirms. The slot rule should be explained before the customer agrees, not after the call is booked. The rule should be written in the booking script.\n\nA preference can also come from a channel the queue does not control. A customer may reply to a message with a time, and that reply may not create a task. Check how each channel hands the preference to the booking system. A channel that only stores a note is a gap. The channel check should include the portal and any automated reply that carries a time."},{"heading":"Decide who can promise the window","body":"Not every agent should be able to promise a narrow window. A new agent may need a supervisor to approve a same-day commitment. Write the authority by contact type and by window length. The rule should be short enough to use during a call. The authority rule should name the longest window an agent may promise without a supervisor's approval.\n\nA promise that requires a specialist should be booked with that specialist's queue, not with the general line. The customer should know which team will call and what will happen if the specialist is unavailable. Do not let the general queue absorb a promise it cannot keep. The specialist queue should confirm that it can accept the task before the agent promises it.\n\nRecord the approver when an exception is made. An exception that is not visible will look like an error later. Review exceptions monthly to see whether the rule is too tight or the demand is changing. The monthly exception review should include the reason and the outcome for each case. The review should name the owner of each rule change.\n\nRecord the customer's time zone by name, not only by an offset. An offset changes with daylight saving, while a zone name stays stable. If the customer does not know the zone, use the number's region as a clue and confirm it. A wrong zone can turn a kept promise into a missed one. The zone name should be stored with the task so a later agent does not recalculate it."},{"heading":"Book the commitment in the system","body":"Create a task with the date, the window, the channel, and the owner. Do not rely on a note that says call back Tuesday. A task can be assigned, measured, and moved when a shift changes. A note can only be read. The task should be visible to the supervisor before the window opens, and not only after the window closes.\n\nCheck the task against the roster before confirming. A window that falls outside the owner's shift is not a commitment the queue can keep. Move the task to a person who is scheduled, or tell the customer the available window. The roster check should include breaks and training, not only the published shift. The check should happen before the customer is told the time.\n\nSet a reminder before the window closes, not after. The reminder should go to the owner and the supervisor. A late reminder turns a manageable task into a missed promise. The reminder should include the customer's number and the question to ask. The reminder should be set when the task is created, not when the shift starts or when someone remembers.\n\nGive the agent a visible limit, such as the number of open commitments they can hold. An agent with twenty promises cannot keep them all, even with good intent. The limit should match the shift and the contact type. A queue that ignores capacity is planning to miss. The capacity limit should be reviewed when the queue adds a channel or a client."},{"heading":"Handle a missed window","body":"If the queue misses the window, contact the customer before they call back. Apologize once, explain the new time, and record the change. Do not blame the customer for not answering a call that was never made. The contact should include a new time the customer can accept, not only an apology. The new time should be confirmed before the call ends.\n\nCheck whether the miss came from a wrong number, a roster change, or a task that was never assigned. Each cause needs a different fix. A pattern of wrong numbers may point to a verification gap; a pattern of unassigned tasks may point to a handoff gap. The cause code should be recorded on the task so the monthly review can count it.\n\nOffer a small, approved remedy when the miss caused a real cost, such as a missed appointment. Do not invent compensation the client has not authorized. Record what was offered and who approved it. The remedy should be approved before the agent offers it, not after the customer asks. The record should show the approval before the customer is told.\n\nBook the task before the call ends. A promise written after the call is more likely to lose a detail or go unassigned. The booking step should be part of the wrap-up, not a separate task for later. If the system is slow, keep the call open until the task is saved. The booking should be saved before the agent says goodbye, even when the call is running long."},{"heading":"Report promise performance","body":"Count the commitments kept, missed, and changed by the customer. Separate a customer-requested change from a queue failure. Report the rate by team and by window length. A narrow window is harder to keep and should be read separately. The rate should show the denominator and the period so a small team is not compared unfairly with a large one.\n\nSample a few missed commitments each month and read the task history. Look for a missing owner, a roster conflict, or a reminder that fired late. Share the pattern with the client without naming customers. The review should end with one process change. The sample should include a customer who changed the time and one who did not answer the call at all.\n\nKeep the report stable over time so the trend is readable. A new definition every month makes the rate meaningless. If the definition must change, mark the break in the chart and explain it. The chart should keep the same scale when the definition changes so the break is visible. The note should say when the new definition took effect.\n\nRecord the customer's response to the missed-window call. A customer who accepts a new time is different from one who asks for a supervisor. The response shapes the next step and the remedy. Do not close the miss until the customer has heard the new plan. The response should be recorded even when the customer asks for no further contact."},{"heading":"Limits and review","body":"This method cannot guarantee that a customer will be available when the queue calls. It also cannot make a narrow window possible on every shift. The queue can control the record, the owner, and the follow-up. State those limits when the client asks for a perfect keep rate. The customer should be told what happens if the queue cannot reach them at the promised time.\n\nReview the authority rule and the booking process each quarter. Test one commitment from booking to close. Update the script when the client changes its hours or channels. Keep the rule short enough to survive a busy shift. The test should include one promise that crosses a shift change and one that stays inside a single shift without a handoff.\n\nCheck the time-zone conversion at every daylight-saving change. A task that was correct in one season can be an hour off in the next. Put the check on the calendar and record the result. A small clock error can break a large promise. The daylight-saving check should include the task reminder and the wording of the customer confirmation message itself.\n\nReport the keep rate for narrow windows separately from wide ones. A queue can look strong overall while failing every two-hour promise. The split shows whether the authority rule matches the demand. It also gives the client a fair basis for changing the rule. The narrow-window rate should be reviewed with the authority rule, because the two limits interact with each other.\n\nKeep the booking template short. A form with too many fields will be skipped under pressure. The fields should match the report the queue needs. The template should ask for the time zone and the channel before it asks for a long note. The template should be tested with an agent during a single busy shift before it goes live."}],"sources":[{"name":"ISO 10002:2018 Quality management - Customer satisfaction - Guidelines for complaints handling","organization":"International Organization for Standardization","date":"2018","url":"https://www.iso.org/standard/71536.html","note":"Guidelines for complaint intake, response, and records in customer-facing organizations."},{"name":"ISO 18295-1:2017 Customer contact centres","organization":"International Organization for Standardization","date":"2017","url":"https://www.iso.org/standard/64739.html","note":"Requirements for customer contact centre processes, people, and performance."},{"name":"NIST Special Publication 800-92: Guide to Computer Security Log Management","organization":"National Institute of Standards and Technology","date":"2006","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/92/final","note":"Guidance on log sources, retention, review, and the limits of log evidence."},{"name":"Occupational Outlook Handbook: Customer Service Representatives","organization":"Bureau of Labor Statistics","date":"2026","url":"https://www.bls.gov/ooh/office-and-administrative-support/customer-service-representatives.htm","note":"Employment, wage, training, and outlook figures for customer service representatives."},{"name":"GAO-20-283G: Assessing Data Reliability","organization":"U.S. Government Accountability Office","date":"2020","url":"https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-20-283g","note":"A practical framework for documenting data sources, testing reliability, and stating limitations."}],"keyStats":["2.67 million U.S. customer service representative jobs in 2025 (BLS)","$21.53 median hourly wage in May 2025 (BLS)","289,500 projected annual openings, 2025 to 2035 (BLS)"],"takeaways":["Record the customer's words and the queue's answer separately.","Turn a promise into a booked task with a date, window, channel, and owner.","Limit who may promise a narrow same-day window.","Separate a customer-requested change from a queue miss in the report.","Recheck time-zone conversion at every daylight-saving change."],"faqs":[{"question":"Is a customer preference the same as a callback promise?","answer":"No. A preference is context until the queue books a task with a window and an owner. Only the booked task is a commitment."},{"question":"What if no one is scheduled during the requested window?","answer":"Offer the nearest window the queue can staff, or route the task to a team that is scheduled. Do not confirm a window the roster cannot cover."}],"related":["callcenteroutsourced-what-does-a-callback-consent-record-need-to-survive-a-complaint-review","callcenteroutsourced-set-a-callback-promise-window-the-queue-can-actually-keep"],"internalLinks":["/research","/services","/contact-us"],"serviceHandoff":{"href":"/contact-us","label":"Review the callback commitment","body":"Bring the current authority rule, the task template, and one month of kept and missed windows so the review can test the promise path."},"image":"/daily22/callcenteroutsourced/callcenteroutsourced-when-does-a-customers-preferred-contact-time-become-a-queue-commitment.webp"}] as unknown as readonly ResearchPost[]
